For any finite type $V$ with decidable equality, simple graph $G$ on $V$, finite set of edges $M \subseteq \mathrm{Sym}^2(V)$, and finite subset of vertices $S \subseteq V$:

If:
1. $M$ is a perfect matching of $G$ (meaning every element of $M$ is an edge of $G$, and every vertex $v \in V$ belongs to a unique edge in $M$);
2. The cardinality of $S$ is even ($|S| \equiv 0 \pmod 2$); and
3. The number of edges in $M$ that cross between $S$ and $S^c$ (i.e., edges in $M$ of the form $s(u, v)$ with $u \in S$ and $v \in S^c$) is non-zero;

Then the number of edges in $M$ crossing between $S$ and $S^c$ is at least $2$.

