For any natural number $k \ge 1$ and any finite set of natural numbers $C$ such that $C$ is a permutation cover for $k$ (meaning every element of $C$ is strictly positive, and every permutation of $\mathrm{Fin}(k)$ has order dividing some element of $C$), there exist two distinct words $u, v \in \mathrm{List}(\mathrm{Fin}(2))$ (i.e., words over $\{0, 1\}$) such that:

1. $u \neq v$;
2. The number of 0s in $u$ equals the number of 1s in $u$;
3. The number of 0s in $v$ equals the number of 1s in $v$;
4. For all permutations $\sigma_0, \sigma_1$ of $\mathrm{Fin}(k)$, evaluating $u$ by substituting $\sigma_0$ for 0 and $\sigma_1$ for 1 and taking their product in the symmetric group on $\mathrm{Fin}(k)$ yields the same permutation as evaluating $v$ in the same way;
5. The length of $u$ equals the length of $v$;
6. The length of $u$ satisfies $\mathrm{length}(u) \le 2 \cdot 3^{|C| - 1} \cdot \sup(C)$ (in $\mathbb{N}$);
7. The natural logarithm of the length of $u$ satisfies $\ln(\mathrm{length}(u)) \le \ln(2) + (|C| - 1)\ln(3) + \ln(\sup(C))$ (in $\mathbb{R}$).

